From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: a733: enable DP alt-mode negotiation, and run the TCPC bus at 400 kHz

Three changes, one goal: let the port find out what a DisplayPort sink on J4
offers. Nothing here carries video -- the PHY's DP personality is not implemented
(`phy-allwinner-a733-combophy-register-a-typec-mux`) and no display driver exists for this family yet.

1. mode-switch on the combo-PHY node. typec_mux_match() looks for exactly this
   property on the remote port parent's fwnode, the same rule that makes the
   existing "orientation-switch" work. Without it the DP alt-mode driver has no
   mux and cannot request a pin assignment.

2. USB-PD on the connector, replacing pd-disable. DP alt mode is negotiated with
   structured VDMs and those need a PD contract, so the reason the property was
   there is answered rather than avoided: tcpm_fw_get_caps() derives pd_supported
   from its absence and then insists on source-pdos, failing probe with -EINVAL
   (measured on the 8 GiB board, 2026-07-21). The PDO is sourced rather than invented:
   `typec-power-opmode = "default"` already sets Rp to USB default, i.e. up to
   900 mA on a USB3-capable port, and PDO_FIXED(5000, 900, USB_COMM) restates
   that in PD terms.

3. clock-frequency = <400000> on r_i2c1. The property was absent, so the
   controller ran at the driver's 100 kHz default. The only device on that bus is
   the Type-C controller, and every PD message body and every ALERT handshake
   crosses it. The vendor DT sets 400 kHz on this same board.

⚠ What this patch does NOT do, and why the record matters more than the tidy
story: a first version of it made the port dual-role with sink-pdos, on the
hypothesis that the monitor hard-resets because it wants to source power and a
source-only port cannot take the sink role. That was built, flashed and
measured -- and refuted. With dual role in place the trace is unchanged:

    PD TX, header: 0x11a1        <- Source_Capabilities
    PD TX complete, status: 2    <- TCPC_TX_FAILED
    ... retried, one gets through with status: 0 ...
    Requested 5000 mV, 900 mA for 900 / 900 mA
    PD TX, header: 0x3a3         <- our Accept
    PD TX complete, status: 2    <- FAILED again
    Received hard reset

So the partner is not refusing a policy; most of our PD transmissions never reach
it. That is a transmit-path problem, which is what change 3 addresses and what
the next measurement has to settle. The dual-role change was reverted rather than
kept "because the vendor does it": a patch whose stated reason is known to be
false does not belong in the tree.

Also ruled out on the way, so nobody re-runs it: the TCPC driver is not the
difference. Mainline's tcpci_rt1711h and the vendor kernel's copy differ by two
lines (the vendor adds etekmicro,et7304), neither knows hynetek,husb311, and both
bind this chip through the richtek,rt1711h fallback with identical init. Separately
worth an upstream note: that init writes PHYCTRL1 0x80 and RTCTRL8/11/14/15/16 at
0x9B/0x9E/0xA1/0xA2/0xA3, all above the highest address the HUSB311 datasheet
defines (0x6F).

dtb built and read back: mode-switch present, source-pdos = <0x0401905a>,
power-role "source", i2c@7084000 clock-frequency = <0x61a80> = 400 kHz,
pd-disable gone, no dual-role and no sink-pdos.

4. altmodes/displayport on the connector, with vdo = <0x00000446>. Without it the
   partner's DP mode is discovered and never entered: measured on the 8 GiB board, the
   partner altmode read `svid=ff01 mode=1 active=no` and typec_mux_set() was never
   called with a DP state, because the port never declared that it supports DP
   itself. The VDO is DFP_D + DP v1.3 signalling + receptacle + **pin assignment C
   only** -- C is the one configuration observed working on this board.

⚠ Why this arrives late, recorded because it cost a measurement: the altmodes node
was first added straight to a working tree that had just been reset for an
apply-check, so the dtb built from it carried altmodes and had silently lost
mode-switch and the 400 kHz property again. Everything measured on that dtb was
measured on a regressed device tree. The lesson is the tree's own: check the
artefact, not the intent.

Assisted-by: Claude

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
@@ -25,6 +25,7 @@
 #include <dt-bindings/reset/sun60i-a733-ccu.h>
 #include <dt-bindings/reset/sun60i-a733-r-ccu.h>
 #include <dt-bindings/reset/sun60i-a733-serdes-ccu.h>
+#include <dt-bindings/usb/pd.h>
 
 / {
 	model = "Radxa Cubie A7S (A733 minimal M1)";
@@ -1435,6 +1436,12 @@
 			 */
 			orientation-switch;
 
+			/* ...and the mux half, for DP alt mode: typec_mux_match()
+			 * looks for "mode-switch" on the remote port parent's
+			 * fwnode (drivers/usb/typec/mux.c).
+			 */
+			mode-switch;
+
 			status = "okay";
 
 			port {
@@ -1946,6 +1953,13 @@
 		 * It exists for exactly one consumer: the Type-C port controller.
 		 */
 		r_i2c1: i2c@7084000 {
+			/* 400 kHz, Fast mode. The property was absent, so the
+			 * controller ran at the 100 kHz default -- and the only
+			 * device on this bus is the Type-C controller, whose PD
+			 * messages and ALERT handshakes all cross it. The vendor
+			 * DT sets 400 kHz here on this same board.
+			 */
+			clock-frequency = <400000>;
 			compatible = "allwinner,sun8i-v536-i2c",
 				     "allwinner,sun6i-a31-i2c";
 			reg = <0x0 0x07084000 0x0 0x400>;
@@ -1998,18 +2012,23 @@
 					data-role = "host";
 					typec-power-opmode = "default";
 					/*
-					 * No USB-PD policy here, and saying so
-					 * is required rather than optional:
-					 * tcpm_fw_get_caps() sets pd_supported
-					 * from the absence of this property and
-					 * then insists on source-pdos, failing
-					 * probe with -EINVAL. Observed on the 8 GiB board,
-					 * 2026-07-21. We only want Type-C
-					 * attach/Rp so a device connects; PD
-					 * would need a power policy this board
-					 * does not have.
+					 * USB-PD, because DisplayPort alt mode is
+					 * negotiated with structured VDMs and those
+					 * need a PD contract. `pd-disable` was here
+					 * for a reason that is now answered rather
+					 * than avoided: tcpm_fw_get_caps() derives
+					 * pd_supported from its absence and then
+					 * insists on source-pdos, failing probe with
+					 * -EINVAL (the 8 GiB board, 2026-07-21).
+					 *
+					 * The PDO is sourced, not invented:
+					 * `typec-power-opmode = "default"` already sets
+					 * Rp to USB default, i.e. up to 900 mA on a
+					 * USB3-capable port, so this restates in PD
+					 * terms what a partner may draw anyway.
 					 */
-					pd-disable;
+					source-pdos = <PDO_FIXED(5000, 900,
+								 PDO_FIXED_USB_COMM)>;
 
 					/* The SS lanes are orientation-mirrored
 					 * inside COMB0, so TCPM has to tell the
@@ -2020,6 +2039,34 @@
 					 * dwc3 only builds a role switch when
 					 * usb-role-switch is set, which it is not.
 					 */
+					/*
+					 * Declare that this port itself supports
+					 * DisplayPort. Without it the partner's DP
+					 * mode is discovered and never entered:
+					 * measured on the 8 GiB board, 2026-08-17, the partner
+					 * altmode read `svid=ff01 mode=1 active=no`
+					 * and typec_mux_set() was never called with a
+					 * DP state.
+					 *
+					 * VDO 0x00000446: [1:0]=10 DFP_D, [5:2]=0001
+					 * DP v1.3 signalling, [6]=1 receptacle,
+					 * [15:8]=0x04 pin assignment C only.
+					 *
+					 * Only C, deliberately: it is the one
+					 * configuration observed working on this
+					 * board (the vendor's DP configure VDM read
+					 * conf 0x406 and the PHY answered with four
+					 * lanes enabled, DP_RAWS_TXDW 0x0FF1).
+					 * Advertising D or E would invite a 2-lane
+					 * split this tree has never exercised.
+					 */
+					altmodes {
+						displayport {
+							svid = /bits/ 16 <0xff01>;
+							vdo = <0x00000446>;
+						};
+					};
+
 					ports {
 						#address-cells = <1>;
 						#size-cells = <0>;
